#{[17592186522662 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522662 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522662 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522662 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186522544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522662 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d57aee12-66ab-51f7-a521-b364c55461d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522690 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522690 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522690 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522690 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522690 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522690 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522690 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522690 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522690 #edb/ref 1089 #edb/ref 17592186522692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522690 #edb/ref 1090 #uuid "9656d62b-8c9d-59f3-888d-dd0b4ff7a7c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522692 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186522829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522692 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186522839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522692 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186522859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522692 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186522873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522692 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186522875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522692 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186522877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522692 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186522883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522692 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186522885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522692 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186522895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522692 #edb/ref 1085 "![](images/52/5270ae4ee5e310d1a28569a26ca5c9adef4c1e3c8af0db159385b0104d160f43.png)\n\nConsider the functions $f(x),$ $g(x),$ and $h(x)$ defined below. A part of the graph of $y=h(x)$ on the interval $x\\in [-3,2]$ is depicted above.\n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\sqrt{x}+2,\\,g(x)=2x-6,\\,h(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3-(x+1)^{2}, & \\,x<0 \\\\ 2x+1, & \\,x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522692 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "562a2fd6-4180-545b-8acd-97ff3b8ce9e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522692 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186522895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522694 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522694 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132986" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522694 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522694 #edb/ref 1071 "$h(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522694 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0286ada6-f474-5c67-9cad-74d4d70fc3df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522694 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $x=4$ is in the interval $x\\ge 0,$ we use the second branch of the piecewise function, namely $h(x)=2x+1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(4) & =2(4)+1 \\\\  & =8+1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522697 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522697 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522697 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132992" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522697 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522697 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $g(x)$ for $x\\in [2,4].$ What is its range over the given domain?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le \\,g(x)\\,\\le $\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522697 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0715be61-dd61-5ecb-88f5-3c63416166d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522697 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we graph the function $g(x)=2x-6.$\n\n![](images/de/de124058817fded27ad4ca9f9537f4a7d09ccd8836c96b0e64580e28dc9ab1ec.png)\n\nThen, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph over the domain $x\\in [2,4].$\n\n* The lowest $y$ value is $-2.$ This occurs at the point $(2,-2).$\n\n* The highest $y$ value is $2.$ This occurs at the point $(4,2).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-2$ and $2,$ including the endpoints.\n\nTherefore, the range is $-2\\le y\\le 2,$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-2\\le g(x)\\le 2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522719 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522719 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132989" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522719 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522719 #edb/ref 1071 "Write the expression for the function $(f+g)(x)$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$(f+g)(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522719 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d43c3ac-d004-5e03-b676-57e7a9141cc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522719 #edb/ref 1074 "We find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(3\\sqrt{x}+2)+(2x-6) \\\\  & =2x+3\\sqrt{x}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522723 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522723 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132985" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522723 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522723 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\n$f(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522723 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "473f318b-f2f7-5bdb-8ff8-b046ab67dc91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522723 #edb/ref 1074 "We substitute $4$ for $x$ in the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\sqrt{x}+2 \\\\ f(4) & =3\\sqrt{4}+2 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+2 \\\\  & =6+2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522736 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522736 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132991" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522736 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522736 #edb/ref 1071 "$(f∘h)(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522736 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c436406-49e2-5628-8484-ed489c154cfd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522736 #edb/ref 1074 "The expression $(f∘h)(4)$ is equivalent to $f(h(4)).$\n\nPreviously, we found that \n\n$$\nh(4)=9.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=9$ into $f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h(4)) & =f(9) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{9}+2 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3+2 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522749 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522749 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132993" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522749 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522749 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $r(x)=\\frac{1}{2}x+k$ is the inverse of $g(x).$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522749 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "84ed963d-febb-5737-ad58-631a391c71a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522749 #edb/ref 1074 "If $r$ is the inverse of $g,$ then \n\n$$\nr(g(x))=x.\n$$\n\nTo find $r(g(x)),$ we write down the function $r$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $g(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}x+k \\\\ r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{2}g(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $g(x)=2x-6$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{2}g(x)+k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x-6)+k \\\\  & =x-3+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we know that $r(g(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =x \\\\ x-3+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-3+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -3+k & =0 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522755 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522755 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132988" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522755 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522755 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the graph of $y=h(x)$ on the interval $[-3,2],$ find the global maximum value of $h(x)$ on this interval.\n\n${h}_{max}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522755 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8db22404-98b1-5e60-b0e3-c4c2f250e097" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522755 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n* The global maximum of a function is the highest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n* A global maximum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $h(x)=5.$ \n\n![](images/70/70c48ae2f451dfb8194a0f93aaaec406c36d381a8d85d2565320c5a7860351e7.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the global maximum is ${h}_{max}=5.$ The function reaches this value when $x=2,$ so the global maximum point is $(2,5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522763 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522763 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522763 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522763 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the root of the function $g(x)?$\n\nThe root is at $x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522763 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9fac61ef-0417-5de3-9911-e04b4f578edc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522763 #edb/ref 1074 "The roots are the solutions to the equation $g(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-6 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522813 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522813 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522813 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522813 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $(f/h)(4).$\n\n$(f/h)(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522813 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe533203-bd0f-5d2e-8bef-eff5c39a0f20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522813 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $(f/h)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $h(x).$ \n\nTo evaluate $(f/h)(4),$ we need to divide $f(4)$ by $h(4).$\n\nEarlier, we found the following values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =8 \\\\ h(4) & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n(f/h)(4)=\\frac{f(4)}{h(4)}=\\frac{8}{9}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522829 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522829 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "263db314-8d23-5342-abb3-bfb253583d4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522829 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522839 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522839 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "49323779-638b-5176-b757-9c95a48376ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522839 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522859 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522859 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8de80ca7-db73-5719-b24a-6b612895db49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522859 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522873 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522873 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc122760-a2dd-5dbc-a5df-976e61603fb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522873 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522875 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c991c6a7-6441-5522-b1e0-1cf796f7d9ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522875 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522877 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522877 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cfb5281e-abf3-5802-9b9b-0ef51c8fc4f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522877 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522883 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522883 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d5b3742f-4311-5078-9311-8d344fbdf850" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522883 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522885 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522885 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dbf1ce6e-c4f4-50db-8aa1-53ed1530b8ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522885 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522891 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eb3cf9c4-c6b1-5cf1-809f-2e668d7344ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522891 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522895 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522895 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f834d06f-11a3-58c8-916e-41aaf6f69787" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522895 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522723 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
